SSC discovery can outlive the first D-Bus claim for the accelerometer.
Clients (mutter/gnome-shell) claim the accelerometer as soon as the
proxy owns its bus name, but the ADSP-backed SSC accel only gets
registered tens of milliseconds later (registry poll + SUID + attribute
round-trips).  At that point handle_generic_method_call() has already
returned the claim with driver_type_exists() FALSE, and no later client
gets polling started either: subsequent claims hit the
"already claimed, no-op" path, and the "appeared while claimed" poll
start only exists in the udev-add hotplug path, which never fires for an
already-present device.

The accel therefore reported HasAccelerometer=true but never delivered a
single measurement, so auto-rotate did nothing.

Start polling any sensor type that was claimed while the proxy was still
rigging itself up, right after its SensorDevice is opened.

diff --git a/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c b/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c
index 2da80bb..a70f9c1 100644
--- a/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c
+++ b/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c
@@ -777,6 +777,14 @@ name_acquired_handler (GDBusConnection *connection,
 		}
 
 		DEVICE_FOR_TYPE(i) = sensor_device;
+
+		/* A sensor type that was claimed while we were still setting up
+		 * (e.g. the SSC accelerometer, whose discovery on the ADSP takes
+		 * a while, is often claimed by a client before it is registered).
+		 * Start polling right now, otherwise the claim is lost and the
+		 * sensor never delivers measurements. */
+		if (g_hash_table_size (data->clients[i]) > 0)
+			driver_set_polling (sensor_device, TRUE);
 	}
 
 	if (!any_sensors_left (data))
